Here’s how you can set it up:

### Step 1: Install Dependencies

First, make sure you have the necessary dependencies installed:
```bash
npm install @react-toolbox/core @types/react @types/node
```

### Step 2: Create Your Context and Custom Hook

Create a context to store the fetched data and a custom hook to handle the fetches.

#### FetchContext.js
```javascript
import { createContext } from "react";

const FetchContext = createContext();

export const useFetch = () => {
    return useContext(FetchContext);
};

export default function FetchProvider({ children }) {
    let [data, setData] = useState(null);

    const fetchData = async (url) => {
        try {
            const response = await fetch(url);
            if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
            const json = await response.json();
            setData(json);
        } catch (err) {
            console.error("Error fetching data:", err.message);
        }
    };

    useEffect(() => {
        // Initialize fetch when the component mounts or when data changes
        fetchData("/api/data");
    }, []);

    return (
        <FetchContext.Provider value={{ data, fetchData }}>
            {children}
        </FetchContext.Provider>
    );
}
```

#### TweakDataHook.js
This hook will allow you to customize the fetched data before setting it into the provider's state.

```javascript
import { useCallback, useState } from "react";
import { useFetch } from "./FetchContext";

// ... import other needed imports ...

export const useTweakFetch = (dataUrl) => {
    const { fetchData } = useFetch();
    const [tweakedData, setTweakedData] = useState(dataUrl ? fetchData(dataUrl) : null);

    const tweakData = useCallback((newData) => {
        if (dataUrl && newData !== dataUrl) {
            setTweakedData(newData);
        }
    }, [dataUrl]);

    return [tweakedData, tweakData];
};
```

### Step 3: Use the Hooks in Your Component

Now, you can use these hooks in any component where you need to fetch and tweak the data.

