One commonly used algorithm for finding the second largest integer element in an unsorted array is the "Two Pass Selection Algorithm". This algorithm works by first selecting the maximum value from the entire array using a selection sort-like technique, and then selecting the second maximum value from the remaining elements after removing the first maximum value.
Here's a sample implementation of this algorithm in C++:
```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int findSecondLargest(int arr[], int n) {
    // First pass: Find the maximum value
    int max1 = INT_MIN;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > max1) {
            max1 = arr[i];
        }
    }

    // Second pass: Remove the maximum value and find the new maximum value
    int max2 = INT_MIN;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] != max1 && arr[i] > max2) {
            max2 = arr[i];
        }
    }

    return max2;
}
```
This function takes an array `arr` and its size `n` as inputs, and returns the second largest integer element in the array. It uses two nested loops to iterate through the array once, making the time complexity of the algorithm O(n). 
To test the function, you can call it with a sample array like this:
```c++
int main() {
    int arr[] = {4, 7, 2, 9, 5};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
    cout << "The second largest element is: " << findSecondLargest(arr, n);
    return 0;
}
```
This will output:
```
The second largest element is: 7
```
Note that this implementation assumes that there is at least one element in the array. If the array is empty or contains only one element, the function may not behave correctly. In such cases, you could add additional checks to handle these edge cases.

To find the second largest integer element in an unsorted array, we can use a modified version of the Quickselect algorithm. This algorithm selects the k-th smallest element from a list of numbers with average-case time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the array.
Here's a C++ implementation of the function:
```
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int secondLargest(int arr[], int n)
{
    // Initialize the first largest and second largest elements
    int first_largest = INT_MIN;
    int second_largest = INT_MIN;

    // Iterate through each element in the array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        // If current element is greater than first largest, update both
        if (arr[i] > first_largest) {
            second_largest = first_largest;
            first_largest = arr[i];
        }

        // If current element is between first largest and second largest,
        // update second largest element
        else if (arr[i] > second_largest && arr[i] != first_largest)
            second_largest = arr[i];
    }

    // Return the second largest element
    return second_largest;
}
int main()
{
    int arr[] = {4, 1, 7, 3, 5};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
    cout << "Second largest element: " << secondLargest(arr, n);
    return 0;
}
```
This function takes an array `arr` and its size `n` as inputs and returns the second largest element in the array. It uses two variables `first_largest` and `second_largest` initialized with the minimum integer value (`INT_MIN`) to store the largest and second largest elements encountered so far during iteration over the array.
For each element in the array, if it is larger than the current `first_largest`, then it becomes the new `first_largest`. If it is smaller than `first_largest` but larger than `second_largest`, then it updates `second_largest`.
Finally, when iterating through the entire array, the second largest element will be stored in `second_largest` variable.

To find the second largest integer element in an unsorted array using C++, we can use a greedy algorithm called "Selection Sort". This algorithm works by repeatedly selecting the smallest element from the unsorted part of the array and swapping it with the first element of the unsorted part until the entire array is sorted.
Here's a function that implements this algorithm:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int secondLargest(int arr[], int n)
{
    int max = INT_MIN; // Initialize maximum variable
    int secMax = INT_MIN; // Initialize second maximum variable

    // Traverse through all elements of array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        // If current element is greater than max,
        if (arr[i] > max)
        {
            secMax = max; // Update second maximum
            max = arr[i]; // Update maximum
        }

        // If current element is between max and secMax,
        else if (arr[i] > secMax && arr[i] != max)
        {
            secMax = arr[i]; // Update second maximum
        }
    }

    return secMax; // Return second maximum value
}
```
This function takes an array `arr` and its size `n` as input parameters. It initializes two variables `max` and `secMax` to represent the maximum and second maximum values respectively. Then, it traverses through all elements of the array and updates these variables accordingly. Finally, it returns the second maximum value.
The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), which means it runs in linear time and is efficient for sorting arrays.
